# USC-II reduction: an exploratory, multi-fuel study

**Completed exploratory reduction and validation — 15 September 2026.**

## Result and intended use

This study constructs new subsets of the supplied USC-II mechanism (111 species, 784 reactions), preserving the surviving rate laws, thermochemistry and transport parameters. The goal is a smaller network for high-temperature ignition and premixed laminar burning velocity across hydrogen/syngas, oxygenated fuel, C1–C4 hydrocarbons and aromatics. No downloaded reduced mechanism is presented as an original design, and no rate constants are fitted to experiments.

| Frozen candidate | Species | Reactions | Species removed | Reactions removed | File |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
| High fidelity | 93 | 553 | 16.2% | 29.5% | [USC-II 93/553](mechanisms/final/uscII-93s-553r.yaml) |
| Balanced | 88 | 475 | 20.7% | 39.4% | [USC-II 88/475](mechanisms/final/uscII-88s-475r.yaml) |
| Compact | 74 | 433 | 33.3% | 44.8% | [USC-II 74/433](mechanisms/final/uscII-74s-433r.yaml) |

File hashes and exact source lineage are pinned in [freeze.json](mechanisms/final/freeze.json). All three passed their declared parent-fidelity limits on the development, confirmation and external sets at the stated numerical settings. Experimental accuracy is a separate, stricter limitation: the parent already has large errors for the selected methanol shock-tube targets.

**88 species / 475 reactions is the recommended strict-tolerance compromise among the candidates tested here.** Across the 202 main conditions at the stated final numerical settings, its largest parent-relative ignition/flame errors are 6.60%/3.49%. The 93/553 option reduces those maxima to 2.74%/1.32%. Moving from 88/475 to 74/433 removes another 14 species and 42 reactions, while increasing the maxima to 15.71%/9.80%; it is appropriate only when the wider 20%/10% fidelity limits are acceptable. Complexity here means network size; a controlled wall-time speedup remains unmeasured.

The comparison is between experimentally measurable macroscopic observables. Species-profile agreement is not an acceptance condition. Parent fidelity measures the effect of deleting chemistry; agreement with measurements measures the combined adequacy of the parent chemistry, retained subset, apparatus approximation and numerical model. These are reported separately.

## Application domain and evidence split

The 17 fuels/blends are H2; methane; methanol; acetylene, ethylene and ethane; allene, propyne, propene and propane; 1,3-butadiene, n-butane and isobutane; benzene and toluene; 50:50 H2/CO; and 85:10:5 methane/ethane/propane (molar). Aromatics and unsaturated fuels were kept even when aggressive reductions failed on them. Nitrogen is an inert diluent, with no NOx chemistry.

| Evidence | Cases | Conditions | Role |
|---|---:|---|---|
| Designed development | 68 ignitions + 34 flames | Ignition: 1100–1600 K, 1–20 atm, phi 0.6–1.6, air or Ar-diluted oxidizer. Flames: 1 atm, phi 0.7/1.4, inlet 300 K (400 K for methanol/aromatics). | Graph sampling, sensitivity ranking, candidate revision and selection. |
| Designed confirmation | 34 ignitions + 17 flames | Ignition: 1200 K/2 atm/phi 0.8 and 1500 K/12 atm/phi 1.1 in air. Flames: 3 atm, phi 1.05, inlet 350/450 K. | Conditions frozen before reduction; predictions withheld from candidate selection. |
| Primary experimental flames | 15 | Park et al.: five C3–C4 fuels, phi 0.8/1/1.4, 298 K, 1 atm. | External comparison with directly tabulated observations. |
| SFCPD curated targets | 20 flames + 10 ignitions | Flames: 298–343 K, 1 atm; ignitions are stoichiometric and Ar-diluted. | External comparison with secondary evaluated targets, often aggregating studies. |
| ChemKED toluene transcription | 4 ignitions | 1434–1635 K, 1.66–2.03 atm; 0.5% toluene/4.5% O2/95% Ar. | OH half-maximum diagnostic; primary article unavailable. |

All 49 external computations together cover 35 flame and 14 ignition entries. The ignition range is 1130–1635 K, 0.95–10.7 atm, phi=1, with 72.7–98 mol% Ar. The flame range is phi 0.701–1.4, 298–343 K, at 1 atm. Two SFCPD butadiene entries duplicate primary Park observations; other aggregate source overlap remains. These are **not 49 independent experiments**, and no independent-study confidence interval is inferred from the point count.

The exact combinations, normalized mole fractions, original units, definitions, uncertainty provenance and source locations are in [the 202-row case catalog](results/case_catalog.csv) and the JSON manifests under `raw/`. The ranges above do not imply that every Cartesian combination was tested. In particular, external observations do not establish lean/rich air-ignition or elevated-pressure flame accuracy. Acetylene, benzene, allene, propyne and the natural-gas blend have only parent-reference validation here. The [fuel/observable coverage table](results/evidence_coverage.md) makes the zeros explicit: external ignition covers methane, methanol, ethylene, ethane, propane, butadiene and toluene; toluene has no external flame comparison. Other externally represented fuels have flame evidence only.

The parent authors describe USC-II as a high-temperature H2/CO/C1–C4 model, with shock-tube ignition, flow-reactor and flame validation. Its aromatic subnetwork is tested here too. This run omits low-temperature/NTC and cool-flame chemistry, wall-loss-sensitive compression ignition, extinction, pollutants/soot, turbulent flames, detonations and liquid evaporation. It makes no claim of a continuous validity envelope.

## How the mechanism was reduced

Cantera 3.2.0 provides established reactor, kinetics and one-dimensional flame solvers. The reduction code is inspectable Python. Initial directed-relation graph with error propagation (DRGEP) scores used maximum-product paths through reaction-production couplings. Active fuel/O2 roots, explicit protection of input fuels and the H2/O2 core, and activity/temperature gates retained 21,648 of 23,632 sampled development states. A normalized absolute **net** reaction-flux score screened reactions; duplicate groups were retained together. Net-flux screening can overlook low-flux but influential pathways, so every candidate was resimulated for the target observables.

Graph-only pruning was insufficient. The search therefore changed to one-species deletion sensitivity from a 108-species/680-reaction connected, flux-filtered baseline. Each of 82 single deletions and the baseline was evaluated on all 102 development conditions. Cumulative deletions were then tested jointly; small individual effects were not assumed to add linearly. Higher reaction-flux thresholds refined promising subsets. Finally, restoring H2C4O and its three surviving reactions repaired a large acetylene ignition error in the 73-species subset, producing the 74-species family.

