**Methanol ignition is a major failure of absolute accuracy.** At 1222 K/10.7 atm the parent predicts 1414.88 µs against a 194.19 µs target (7.29 times, +628.61%); at 1130 K/10.43 atm it predicts 6733.25 µs against 529.39 µs (12.72 times, +1171.89%). All reduced models inherit essentially the same discrepancy. The mixture, units and maximum-pressure-gradient definition were checked against the primary methanol methods. No input or diagnostic mismatch was identified, but absent facility histories and further kinetic analysis prevent assigning a specific chemical or apparatus cause. These mechanisms should not be treated as experimentally accurate methanol ignition models over those conditions.

The parent also overpredicts the two lower-temperature toluene delays by about 55% and 84%. Some reduced results move closer to selected observations by offsetting parent bias; that is not evidence of general improvement or successful calibration. No rates were fitted. The primary flame median errors remain around 6–7%, while maxima reach 17–21%; only 4–6 of the 15 points fall within the reported sampling intervals. Thus the useful result is a smaller network with controlled added error, with **limited and uneven absolute experimental accuracy**.

See the [complete point comparisons](results/external_final_comparison/comparison.csv), [evidence-class summaries](results/external_final_comparison/summary.json), and [fuel-specific summaries](results/external_by_fuel.csv). Standalone figures show [primary flames](figures/primary_flames.svg), [all external ignition ratios](figures/external_ignition_ratios.svg), and [toluene delays](figures/toluene_ignition.svg).

The strongest directly checked primary source is Park et al. (2016), DOI [10.1016/j.combustflame.2016.07.004](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.combustflame.2016.07.004), including its publisher-hosted supplementary Table S1. Counterflow twin-flame PIV measurements were nonlinearly extrapolated to zero strain with computational assistance. Their listed 2-sigma speed uncertainties are 0.8–1.5 cm/s at the selected points; phi uncertainty is within 1%. These quantify reported sampling uncertainty, not all extrapolation and interlaboratory systematics. Fuel/air mixtures are reconstructed using dry O2:N2=1:3.76; individual shot compositions were not supplied. Planar adiabatic flames with multicomponent transport and Soret approximate this zero-strain observable, rather than the finite-strain apparatus flow. The paper's modified 'prior USC' model and updated transport differ from the supplied parent, so its published USC curves are not claimed to be reproduced exactly.

For selected methanol shock-tube targets, Burke et al. (2016), DOI [10.1016/j.combustflame.2015.11.004](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.combustflame.2015.11.004), confirms maximum dP/dt and the 5.70/8.55/85.75 mol% methanol/O2/Ar mixture. Its RCM uncertainty is not reassigned to shock-tube measurements. For butadiene, Zhou et al. (2018), DOI [10.1016/j.combustflame.2018.08.006](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.combustflame.2018.08.006), describes several facilities and diagnostics; pressure-gradient versus tangent-onset ambiguity is retained explicitly. Ideal constant volume does not reproduce measured facility pressure histories. ChemKED supplies the toluene OH half-maximum definition and ±10% delay, ±1% temperature uncertainties, with unspecified confidence level; the primary Vasudevan et al. article was not obtained. SFCPD target uncertainties are secondary assessments and sometimes include generic floors. Agreement with them is labeled target consistency, not primary measurement reproduction.

The OH diagnostic is an explicitly qualified mole-fraction proxy: the transcription specifies OH half maximum without identifying the measured signal quantity. Replacing mole fraction by a concentration/number-density proxy proportional to `X_OH P/T` changes the 16 parent/final long-window delays by at most **0.00290%**. Extending integration to 20 ms changes the normal-window OH delay by at most **0.0000108%** across all four models. Reducing initial temperature by 1% lengthens delay by 20.79–23.53%; increasing it by 1% shortens delay by 16.59–18.97%. These sensitivity calculations are neither new observations nor a combined confidence interval. All 56 external pressure-derivative peaks are interior to their trajectories. For the two butadiene points, pressure-tangent onset is 3.01–7.07% earlier than the gradient peak across the models. See [diagnostic checks](results/diagnostic_checks.json) and [OH quantity check](results/OH_quantity_proxy_check.json).

## Numerical and physical consistency

Ignition uses a homogeneous adiabatic fixed-volume ideal-gas reactor. Synthetic fidelity uses maximum dT/dt; external comparisons use the recorded pressure or OH diagnostic. Final reactor tolerances are relative 1e-10, absolute 1e-20, with explicit post-ignition stopping and interior-peak checks. Development and confirmation flames use mixture-averaged transport without Soret. External flames use multicomponent transport with Soret. No radiation or heat loss is included.

Final flame comparisons use ratio=3, slope=0.01, curve=0.02 refinement criteria. Earlier coarse grids were tightened in stages. For all 68 parent ignitions, normal-to-tight integration changed delay by at most 0.0375%. Parent flame changes over all 34 cases were at most 2.881% from coarse to the first refinement, 2.084% at the next refinement, and 1.055% from 0.02/0.04 to 0.01/0.02. Eight representative parent flames additionally refined to 0.005/0.01 changed by at most 0.552% (median 0.410%). Six independent-start/wider-domain checks changed the corresponding fine speeds by at most 0.422%; these combined perturbations do not isolate domain width from initialization effects.

At the compact candidate's limiting rich butadiene flame, matched refinement from 0.01/0.02 to 0.005/0.01 changed its parent-relative error from **9.5894% to 9.5862%**, only −0.00323 percentage points. Parent speed changed from 0.4215821 to 0.4217958 m/s (1343 to 2610 points); the compact speed changed from 0.4620092 to 0.4622298 m/s (1408 to 2365 points). A 900 s parent attempt timed out; a longer retry completed in 1033 s. The analogous rich-toluene check changed the balanced model's error from −3.4949% to −3.4379%. These checks support the tested threshold decisions without establishing a rigorous global discretization bound. See [butadiene](results/compact_boundary_convergence.json) and [toluene](results/aromatic_boundary_convergence.json) paired results.

For the 32 external parent flames with successful coarse/fine pairs, refinement to 0.01/0.02 changed speed by at most 2.187% (median 1.374%). An additional multicomponent/Soret H2 refinement to 0.005/0.01 changed speed by 0.0278%. For the rich n-butane primary flame, the staged 0.005/0.01 parent solve converged in 3003 s on 1859 points, changing speed from 0.21729395 to 0.21823127 m/s (+0.431%). Its converged intermediate 0.0075/0.015 grid changed speed by +0.273%; an earlier direct 1800 s attempt timed out. At the compact model's limiting external rich-butadiene flame, matched multicomponent/Soret refinement changed its parent-relative error from **9.7996% to 9.7373%**, a −0.06234 percentage-point shift, still below 10%. The parent converged on 2596 points at 0.41287270 m/s; the compact model on 2393 points at 0.45307535 m/s. The intermediate paired grid gave 9.7582%. These checks support the finite-grid result while leaving the compact model a narrow margin; they do not establish an asymptotic or domain-wide error bound. The complete [supplementary grid results](results/final_grid_checks.json) retain both intermediate and final stages.

Every retained reaction is exactly element balanced. Surviving species composition, NASA coefficients and transport fields are unchanged. At seven checked temperatures (298–3800 K), retained net reaction rates agree with the parent at identical embedded states to within 2.78e-13 relative difference, and equilibrium constants within 1.44e-14. Analytic polynomial-extremum checks find positive heat capacities over 298–3800 K (minimum Cp/R about 2.5), including the explicitly qualified extrapolated portions.

