Preserving original NASA coefficients does not cure inherited problems. Five parent species trigger segment-continuity warnings; the parent enthalpy joins for C4H5-2 and C4H6-2 differ by about 6.52 and 4.41 kJ/mol. Some aromatic intermediate fits terminate below the simulated temperature. The hottest rich acetylene/allene/propyne ignition cases exceed 3500 K (up to about 3750 K); the maximum instantaneous mass fraction outside constituent fit ranges is 30.46% for acetylene and about 30.2% for allene/propyne. They are extrapolation stress tests, not thermochemically validated predictions. They remain in the reported error statistics, with this qualification. Some 298 K flame inlets are also 2 K below a 300 K lower fit bound. These inherited limitations remain in relevant reduced models. Detailed audits are linked in [METHODS.md](METHODS.md).

## What the retrieved evidence changed

- The USC-II scope and validation page motivated retaining hydrogen/CO, C1–C4 and aromatic fuel families, rather than choosing methane alone.
- Public SFCPD and ChemKED access provided exact observation definitions and mixtures. ReSpecTh's registration barrier and unavailable primary articles limited coverage; no inaccessible source was treated as read.
- Park's primary paper and supplementary table added directly tabulated C3–C4 flame evidence and established the counterflow extrapolation, uncertainty and transport qualifications. Methanol and butadiene primary methods changed the selected diagnostic interpretation and prevented misuse of RCM or emission uncertainties.
- pyMARS and the Niemeyer–Sung graph-method study supported the maximum-product implementation and a sensitivity stage. Measured aromatic/butadiene failures, rather than literature compression ratios, triggered the change from uniform graph thresholds to deletion sensitivity.
- Cantera's reaction-reduction example supported net-flux screening as a candidate heuristic; full macroscopic tests determined acceptance. Numerical convergence evidence required finer grids and additional checks near the compact threshold.
- The refined compact boundary check supported freezing the smaller 433-reaction version. Strongly pruning it to 394 reactions had exceeded the flame limit. The search then stopped changing chemistry and shifted to independent assessment; this is a local exploratory trade-off, not an optimality claim.
- The independent confirmation set retained the declared fidelity limits. External comparisons exposed large inherited methanol ignition bias and uneven flame agreement, limiting the recommendation to a reduction with controlled added error. Those findings did not trigger rate fitting, deletion of difficult validation cases or changes to the frozen files. The external compact flame near its tolerance boundary prompted an additional numerical check.

## Reproduction, failures and limitations

[README.md](README.md) gives commands and the archive layout; [METHODS.md](METHODS.md) documents full methods and source qualifications. Exact solver inputs, original reaction indices, downloaded bytes, URL/status/hash records, scalar results, profiles and tracebacks are retained. Small tar archives may remain unextracted for screening batches; their `summary.json` files contain the full scalar outputs. Offline numerical wheels and package versions are supplied. Warm-start profiles are initial guesses only; the solver remains free to change temperature, composition and speed.

An early four-candidate remote job settled without recoverable output. Some large initial archives were only partly collected, requiring recovery of parent trajectories; an output-size limit is the suspected cause rather than a demonstrated scientific failure. The first fine external batch also ended with incomplete archives and a mixture of successful solves and timeouts; its cause was not fully diagnosed. Later jobs used incremental scalar checkpoints, smaller closed archives, direct multicomponent warm starts and explicit longer per-case limits. Failures are retained as failures, not counted as chemical validation or hidden from final success counts.

The final main validation contains **808 successful results** (202 conditions × parent plus three reduced models), with no unresolved failed or missing required case. The 48 OH-window/temperature perturbations also succeed. A parent external rich-isobutane flame that timed out at 1200 s was recovered with staged refinement in 719 s; the 3-atm parent isobutane confirmation flame was recovered with a fresh, staged solve in 392 s. Original failed attempts remain alongside these successful results. The direct extra-fine rich n-butane attempt timed out at 1800 s; its staged retry succeeded in 3003 s within a 3600 s limit. The paired external rich-butadiene refinement also succeeded, in 1670 s for the parent and 477 s for the compact model. All planned supplementary targets now have completed results. All 42 remote jobs are settled, including the early job whose outputs were unavailable; its scientific cases were recomputed in recoverable batches. The [execution ledger](raw/remote_job_ledger_snapshot.json) preserves the final job metadata. Self-review and archive registration are bookkeeping, not independent scientific certification.

The search stops with three quantified size/fidelity choices, a failed stronger-pruning example, completed independent-condition assessment and explicit external-accuracy limits. Further reduction or rate calibration would require a new search and fresh confirmation evidence.

This is a resource-limited exploratory search. Global minimality remains untested, and parameter preservation is not an experimental accuracy certificate. Sparse condition coverage, absent external observations for five fuels/blends, missing lean/rich air-ignition and high-pressure-flame evidence, uncertain facility corrections, inherited thermochemical problems and residual numerical error bound the conclusions.
